    "q-132249": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132249",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=2x,$ $y=6-x,$ and $x=0,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/24d3b93e82e88bb581eff3005687ae4ee3dc3ae0d6c25fbc26f72665042ae5c6.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+6 & =2x \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14261478/assets/af61865f54494840090351296e8a1c21446787ca0b273005e7bf9c2907585adb.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2x\\le y\\le 6-x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{6-x}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-132249-a",
