    "q-37683": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37683",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$",
      "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{1/x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-x]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}+1-(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{11}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
