    "q-132288": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132288",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Calculate ${∬}_{D}(1-y)\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}(1-y)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}(1-y)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}(1-y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\sqrt{x}-3x+{x}^{2})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\sqrt{x}-3x+{x}^{2})dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{1/2}-3x+{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{3}{2}(1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
